; Under the sanitize profile rig's executables link the debug runtime and
; stress the collector, so a value a stub holds unrooted, or memory a
; finaliser frees while it is still used, fails at once. OCAMLRUNPARAM gives a
; 32 KiB minor heap (s=4k), major cycles far more often (o=20), pushed at once
; by custom blocks (M=1, m=1), and a heap check at the end of each (V=1); v=0
; silences the debug runtime's banner, which a cram test would read as output.
;
; Every profile compiles the C with WINPTHREADS_USE_DLLIMPORT, which only
; mingw-w64's pthread.h and sched.h read (from mingw-w64 11). The OCaml runtime
; links winpthreads statically, so a stub DLL that a bytecode program or a
; toplevel loads takes pthread_* from the executable, which flexdll links at
; load time. A direct call reaches 2 GiB, and Windows may load a DLL farther
; than that from the executable, where flexdll refuses it ("cannot relocate
; pthread_mutex_unlock RELOC_REL32, target is too far"). Declared dllimport,
; each call goes through a pointer, which reaches any address, as calls to the
; runtime's own functions do through CAMLextern. A program linked whole fills
; the pointers at link time.

(env
 (sanitize
  (c_flags
   (:standard -DWINPTHREADS_USE_DLLIMPORT))
  (link_flags
   (:standard -runtime-variant d))
  (env-vars
   (OCAMLRUNPARAM s=4k,o=20,M=1,m=1,V=1,v=0)))
 (_
  (c_flags
   (:standard -DWINPTHREADS_USE_DLLIMPORT))))

; README.md's program runs under mdx, off under sanitize and tsan as the root
; dune says.

(mdx
 (files README.md)
 (package rig)
 (enabled_if
  (and
   (<> %{profile} sanitize)
   (<> %{profile} tsan)))
 (libraries rig unix))
